.TH th_read 3 "Jan 2001" "University of Illinois" "C Library Calls"
.SH NAME
th_read, th_write \- read and write a file header block from a tar archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <libtar.h>
.P
.BI "int th_read(TAR *" t ");"

.BI "int th_write(TAR *" t ");"
.SH VERSION
This man page documents version 1.2 of \fBlibtar\fP.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBth_read\fP() function reads the next logical record from the tar
archive associated with the \fITAR\fP handle \fIt\fP.  It then sets the
current tar header associated with \fIt\fP to the contents of the block
read.

A logical record consists of zero or more GNU \fBL\fP (long name) and
\fBK\fP (long link) extension records, in either order, each followed by
its complete payload blocks, and finally one ordinary header.  When the
same extension type occurs more than once before an ordinary header, the
last one wins.  Extension names apply only to the immediately following
ordinary header and are discarded at the start of the next
\fBth_read\fP() call.  A logical record is reported only once the ordinary
header has been read completely and validated; an extension chain that is
followed by end-of-archive, a zero block or a truncated header is an
error.

The declared length of an extension payload is taken from its 12-byte
size field.  Only octal digits, optionally surrounded by spaces or NULs,
are accepted; a value that cannot be represented is rejected.  The
payload must contain either no NUL byte or a single NUL as its last
declared byte.  A NUL occurring earlier, or a zero declared length, is an
error.  Block padding past the declared length is not part of the name.

By default a single logical record may carry at most 1 MiB of declared
extension payload and 64 extension headers.  Use
\fBtar_set_gnu_extension_limits\fP() to change these bounds.  Exceeding a
bound fails before the offending payload is read.

The \fBth_write\fP() function writes the contents of the current
tar header associated with \fIt\fP to the tar archive associated
with \fIt\fP.
.SH "ENTRY VIEW LIFETIME"
The names reported for one logical record, including strings returned by
\fBth_get_pathname\fP() and \fBth_get_linkname\fP() for GNU extensions,
belong to the handle and are valid only until the next \fBth_read\fP()
call or \fBtar_close\fP().  The next \fBth_read\fP() invalidates the
previous view before it reads any block, even if it then fails.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On successful completion, \fBth_read\fP() and \fBth_write\fP() will
return 0.  On failure, they will return \-1 and set \fIerrno\fP to an
appropriate value.

On \fIEOF\fP, \fBth_read\fP() will return 1.

After a failed \fBth_read\fP() the handle is in a read-error state: only
\fBtar_close\fP() may be called, and this includes a repeated
\fBth_read\fP(), which returns the original error without contacting the
reader.
.SH ERRORS
\fBth_read\fP() and \fBth_write\fP() will fail if:
.IP \fBEINVAL\fP
Less than \fBT_BLOCKSIZE\fP blocks were read or written, a GNU extension
chain was malformed or dangling, or an extension size field was invalid.
.IP \fBE2BIG\fP
A GNU extension chain exceeded its configured byte or header limit.
.IP \fBENOMEM\fP
An extension name could not be allocated.
.PP
If the reader function returns \-1, \fBth_read\fP() fails with the
\fIerrno\fP it set.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tar_set_gnu_extension_limits (3),
.BR tar_block_read (3),
.BR tar_block_write (3)
